From 6a5d93a3b09a57bfa6d0a798d0771f654582fdc1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: David <david@katalystnord.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 18:59:44 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] kv-stream: split attention, the CPU attends the oldest
 streamed pages in place

LLAMA_KV_SPLIT=<fraction> (off by default): in single-token decode steps the CPU
takes that share of each layer's streamed pages, the oldest ones after the
resident prefix, and attends over them in the host store; those pages are not
copied. Per layer the host fetches Q and the mask slice (cudaMemcpyAsync and an
event), a worker thread runs ggml's CPU flash attention with its LSE while the GPU
attends over the rest, and the GPU merges the CPU's output into the resumed vector
result (split ops: fetch, wait, merge). Only when pages stream and the resume plan
is the vector kernel; otherwise nothing changes.

LLAMA_KV_SPLIT_THREADS (default two thirds of the hardware threads),
LLAMA_KV_SPLIT_DEBUG (timings every 32 steps), LLAMA_KV_SPLIT_VERIFY (compares
every decode layer with CPU attention over the whole context).

Qwen3.5 4B at 130K on brick (q8_0 K, q4_0 V): 8.66 -> 10.15 t/s at 0.5 with 6
threads; 0.7 is slower than no split. Verify at 130K: mean |diff| 5.22e-5 with the
split against 5.36e-5 without.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
